-- title:
--   Definitions 2.2, 2.5 and §3.3 — the lattice of a family, its dual within the span, $\lambda_{\mathrm{target}}$, $Z_{\mathrm{target}}$
-- statement:
--   For vectors $b_1,\dots,b_m\in\mathbb R^d$, $\Lambda$ is the set of their integer combinations and $V$ its real span. The **dual lattice within $V$** is
--   $$
--   \Lambda^*=\{w\in V:\langle w,v\rangle\in\mathbb Z\ \text{for every}\ v\in\Lambda\}.
--   $$
--   For a target vector $v_{\mathrm{tar}}$ and $p>0$,
--   $$
--   \lambda_{\mathrm{target}}=\min\{\|w\|_2:w\in\Lambda^*,\ \langle v_{\mathrm{tar}},w\rangle\ne0\},\qquad Z_{\mathrm{target}}=\max\{1,(p\lambda_{\mathrm{target}})^{-1}\}.
--   $$
--   $Z_{\mathrm{target}}$ controls the number of final intervals per coordinate in the recovery search.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The vectors are indexed by any finite type, and linear independence is a hypothesis of each theorem. The minimum is written as an infimum of norms. On an empty set this infimum is $0$, and then $Z_{\mathrm{target}}=1$; the milestone on the target dual minimum proves that for a nonzero lattice vector the set is nonempty and the infimum is attained and positive, and a sanity check computes it on a one-dimensional lattice.
-- source:
--   Kang, Lee, Provable Recovery from Integer Quotients: Improved Bounds for Nonlinear Hidden Number Problems, STOC 2027 submission (manuscript sha256 605f9dd3…), Definition 2.2, p.14 (PDF p.15); Definition 2.5, p.15 (PDF p.16); §3.3, p.19 (PDF p.20)

import Mathlib

namespace IntQuotHNP

open InnerProductSpace

/-- **The lattice generated by a family of vectors** (Definition 2.2).
Kang, Lee, *Provable Recovery from Integer Quotients*, STOC 2027 submission (manuscript sha256
605f9dd3…), Definition 2.2, p.14 (PDF p.15): "Let b₁, . . . , b_m ∈ ℝ^d be linearly independent over
ℝ, where 1 ≤ m ≤ d. … The lattice generated by B is Λ = {∑ α_i b_i : α₁, . . . , α_m ∈ ℤ} ⊆ ℝ^d."

`latticeOf b` is the set of integer combinations of the vectors `b i`.

**Formalization Note.** The family is indexed by any finite type `ι` and lives in the Euclidean
space `EuclideanSpace ℝ κ` (`ℝ^d` with `d = |κ|`). Linear independence, which makes `b` a basis of
the lattice, is a hypothesis of each theorem, not part of this definition. -/
def latticeOf {ι κ : Type*} [Fintype κ] (b : ι → EuclideanSpace ℝ κ) :
    Submodule ℤ (EuclideanSpace ℝ κ) :=
  Submodule.span ℤ (Set.range b)

/-- **The dual lattice within the span** (Definition 2.5).
Kang, Lee, STOC 2027 submission (manuscript sha256 605f9dd3…), Definition 2.5, p.15 (PDF p.16):
"The dual lattice of Λ within V is Λ∗ = {w ∈ V : ⟨w, v⟩ ∈ ℤ for every v ∈ Λ}." Here `V = span_ℝ Λ`.

**Formalization Note.** `w` must lie in the real span of the `b i`, not in the whole ambient space:
for a lattice of rank `m < d` the two duals differ. -/
def dualLattice {ι κ : Type*} [Fintype κ] (b : ι → EuclideanSpace ℝ κ) :
    Set (EuclideanSpace ℝ κ) :=
  {w | w ∈ Submodule.span ℝ (Set.range b) ∧ ∀ v ∈ latticeOf b, ∃ z : ℤ, ⟪w, v⟫_ℝ = z}

/-- **The target dual minimum `λ_target`** (§3.3).
Kang, Lee, STOC 2027 submission (manuscript sha256 605f9dd3…), §3.3, p.19 (PDF p.20): "Define
λ_target = min{∥w∥₂ : w ∈ Λ∗, ⟨v_tar, w⟩ ≠ 0}".

**Formalization Note.** The paper's `min` is written as `sInf` of the set of norms. That the set
is nonempty and the infimum is attained and positive (so that Lean's `sInf ∅ = 0` never applies) is
part of the milestone `target_dual_minimum`, as it is part of the paper's text. -/
noncomputable def lambdaTarget {ι κ : Type*} [Fintype κ] (b : ι → EuclideanSpace ℝ κ)
    (v : EuclideanSpace ℝ κ) : ℝ :=
  sInf {r : ℝ | ∃ w ∈ dualLattice b, ⟪v, w⟫_ℝ ≠ 0 ∧ ‖w‖ = r}

/-- **The quantity `Z_target`** (§3.3).
Kang, Lee, STOC 2027 submission (manuscript sha256 605f9dd3…), §3.3, p.19 (PDF p.20):
"Z_target = max{1, (pλ_target)⁻¹}."

**Formalization Note.** The modulus `p` enters as a real number. -/
noncomputable def zTarget {ι κ : Type*} [Fintype κ] (b : ι → EuclideanSpace ℝ κ)
    (v : EuclideanSpace ℝ κ) (p : ℝ) : ℝ :=
  max 1 (p * lambdaTarget b v)⁻¹

end IntQuotHNP


